---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';
import { FORECASTS_ENABLED } from '../lib/flags';
import { FILLED_TESTS, HELD_OUT_TEST, LATEST_FILLED_TEST } from '../lib/data/accuracy';
import { dataMeta, getForecastMeta } from '../lib/data';
import { FORECAST_RUNS } from '../lib/sim/forecast';
import { eS, pct } from '../components/course/fmt';
import AccuracyChart from '../components/home/AccuracyChart.svelte';
import Formula from '../components/method/Formula.astro';
const bars = [60, 75, 60, 42, 28, 18, 12, 8, 5, 3];
const notes = [
  ['Chance of a seat', ''],
  ['Chance it fills', 'If not, the minimum bid gets in'],
  ['Bid needed', 'Lowest winning bid'],
];
const leads = [[450, 120], [112, 210], [450, 290]];
const accounts = [
  ['Seats against bidders', 'The chance depends on how many people bid for the seats, not on one typical bid.'],
  ['One seat or many', 'With one seat left you must beat nearly every other bid. With many, the cutoff sits near the minimum.'],
  ['Bids bunch at the minimum', 'Many bid the minimum or just above it. The forecast keeps that pile, so a section that does not fill clears at the minimum.'],
  ['Professor and time slot', 'Each is read from that professor\'s history. Early, evening and Friday sections get their own adjustment per window.'],
];
const swap = [
    'Swap mode on a course page shows how likely a swap from your section into each other section is. The chance depends on how much more or less wanted the other section is than yours. Wantedness is the log of the predicted Round 1 Window 1 clearing bid, the first bidding window after the swap round, from the same simulation as the bids on the page (one person, the section\'s full seat count). That already reflects day, time and instructor. Many sections clear at the minimum bid, so ties are broken by expected bidders per seat, which counts for a quarter as much as the bid on a log scale. Where a section has no such forecast, it uses last term\'s clearing bids for the same instructor (or the same day and start time), compared with the course\'s other sections.',
    'Pre-assigned courses (including COR1100, COR2100 and COR3001) are different. When the median share of a section\'s seats offered in Round 1 Window 1 has been under 25%, nearly everyone is placed before bidding and the simulation would give every section the minimum bid. For these we skip it and use the forecast bid level for the section\'s instructor and time slot (early, mid or late, and Friday). The people holding a section are its seats minus the seats usually offered in bidding.',
    'The model is a direct pair: you get section B only if someone in B swaps into your section A, and other people in A who want B compete for the same people. Cycles through three or more sections are ignored, so the chance is, if anything, understated. Each holder applies to swap with a probability of 10% times a factor that grows with how much more wanted the destination is, so students lean towards the pricier section and a swap into one is harder.',
    'The two settings, the 10% and how sharply preference follows price, are our judgment, not fitted to swap outcomes, because SMU publishes none. They are set so the typical chance matches what we expect: about 60 to 70% when the other section is about as wanted as yours (a gap of 0.03 or less in log bid), 80 to 90% when it is clearly less wanted (a gap of 0.12 to 0.25 the cheap way), 20 to 40% when more wanted (0.15 to 0.25), and under 15% when much more wanted (0.40 or more). Those gaps are taken from the spread within courses: among courses bid for normally, the typical gap between two sections of one course is 0.10, and a quarter of pairs differ by more than 0.28. In between, the chance moves smoothly.',
    'The tooltip shows a rough percentage, rounded to the nearest 5, and a range from varying the two settings by a moderate amount (the 10% by half as much again either way, the sharpness by a quarter). Likely means a central chance of 75% or more, Unlikely means under 40%, and Possible is in between. The range does not change the label.',
    'Limits: none of this has been tested against real swaps, and no student-submitted data is used. The percentages are rough guides for ranking your options, not probabilities. The sample size shown is the number of past results behind the bid prediction.',
];
const snipe = [
    'Drop and re-bid is for a class you won earlier with a high bid. You drop it in a later window, get a refund, and bid the minimum again, hoping nobody else takes the freed seat. SMU\'s own blog lists it as a strategy.',
    'The refund is what you paid, up to a cap by round: e$50 in Rounds 1 and 1A, e$40 in 1B, e$30 in 2, e$10 in 2A. We read "up to what you paid" ourselves; the sources give only the cap. Saving is the refund minus the re-bid, and the cost if it works is what you paid, minus the refund, plus the re-bid.',
    'A seat dropped in a window opens in that window, so anyone bidding can take it. The chance it works is the chance a bid of your re-bid wins one seat for one bidder in that window, from the same simulation and forecast as the bids on the course page. Bidding is blind, so nobody knows who else will go for the seat.',
    'Limits: the simulation does not know that a freed seat may draw fewer bidders than a seat offered at the window\'s start, or more because dropped seats are watched. It is a rough guide. If it fails you lose the class and keep only the refund.',
];
const drop = [
    'Drop chance is the chance that a section that is full now is offered seats again in a later window of the same term, shown on the course page as "Seat may open". It counts past cases of the same course (the same class number, same term) that were full in the same window, and checks whether any later window up to the one shown offered seats again. A case that was full when the window opened and one that sold out during it behave differently, so only the matching kind is counted: while a window is open, sections that started it full; between windows, sections that ended the last window full.',
    'A course with few cases borrows from its subject, and a subject with few from all courses: its own rate is blended with the parent\'s as if the parent had contributed 5 extra cases. The sample size in the tooltip is the number of past cases at the narrowest level with at least 5, and the chance by each later window never goes down.',
    'Limits: the data records that seats were offered again, not why (a drop, a bigger quota, or seats released at the start of a round), and most of the chance comes from the start of Round 2. It does not say whether you can win the seat, and it ignores things we cannot see, such as exams or a lecturer change. Sections of a course in one term are not independent, so the sample size overstates how much evidence there is. Only collected BOSS results are used. It is a rough guide, not a promise.',
];
// Figures come from lib/data/accuracy.ts (generated from the backtest, shared with the home strip). All-forecast figures for
// 2025-26 Term 2 come from forecast-meta.json; 2026-27 Term 1 (held out) comes from accuracy.json.
const fm = getForecastMeta();
const tests = [
  {
    ...FILLED_TESTS[0],
    n: fm.samples, baseN: fm.baseline?.samples ?? fm.samples,
    mae: fm.maeCents ?? null, base: fm.baseline?.maeCents ?? null, cov: fm.coverage80 ?? null,
  },
  {
    ...FILLED_TESTS[1],
    term: `${HELD_OUT_TEST.term} (held out)`,
    n: HELD_OUT_TEST.n, baseN: HELD_OUT_TEST.baseN, mae: HELD_OUT_TEST.mae, base: HELD_OUT_TEST.base, cov: HELD_OUT_TEST.cov,
  },
];
// Headline figures: the latest test (held-out term), sections that filled.
const latest = LATEST_FILLED_TEST;
const e = (c: number | null) => (c === null ? '–' : eS(c));
const p = (v: number | null) => (v === null ? '–' : pct(v));
const { sourceRows: results, sourceTerms: terms } = dataMeta as { sourceRows?: number; sourceTerms?: number };
const bt = fm.windows.map((w) => ({ w, ...fm.byWindow[w] }));
---
<Base title="How the forecast works · Bidwise" active="method">
  <header class="head">
    <h1>How the forecast works</h1>
    {results && terms && <p class="src">{terms} terms, {results.toLocaleString('en-US')} results, BOSS Overall Results, Nov 2026</p>}
  </header>

  {FORECASTS_ENABLED ? (<>
  <section class="hero">
    <svg viewBox="40 10 880 350" role="img" aria-label="Your chance of a seat rises with your bid; below it, a histogram of the bid needed, with a spike at the minimum bid and a long right tail">
      <g stroke="var(--rule-strong)" stroke-width="1.5" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke">
        <line x1="60" y1="190" x2="900" y2="190" />
        <line x1="60" y1="340" x2="900" y2="340" />
        <line x1="60" y1="20" x2="60" y2="190" />
      </g>
      <line x1="112" y1="20" x2="112" y2="340" stroke="var(--rule-strong)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 4" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />
      <path d="M112 185 C240 183 340 170 450 120 S720 35 880 30" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />
      <g fill="var(--muted)" fill-opacity="0.45">
        <rect x="100" y="210" width="24" height="130" rx="1" />
        {bars.map((h, i) => <rect x={130 + i * 56} y={340 - h} width="46" height={h} rx="1" />)}
      </g>
      <line x1="450" y1="120" x2="450" y2="340" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 4" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />
      <g class="mk" text-anchor="middle">
        {leads.map((p, i) => (
          <g>
            <circle cx={p[0]} cy={p[1]} r="12" fill="var(--accent)" />
            <text x={p[0]} y={p[1] + 4.5} font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="var(--accent-ink)">{i + 1}</text>
          </g>
        ))}
      </g>
    </svg>
    <ol class="legend">
      {notes.map((n) => <li><b>{n[0]}</b>{n[1] && <span>{n[1]}</span>}</li>)}
    </ol>
  </section>
  </>) : null}

  {FORECASTS_ENABLED && (<>
  <section class="plain" id="plain" aria-label="Summary">
    <div class="card">
      <h2>What it predicts</h2>
      <ul class="bul">
        <li>The bid needed for a seat, with a range</li>
        <li>The chance that a given bid gets one</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h2>What it learns from</h2>
      <ul class="bul">
        <li>Past BOSS results: seats offered, seats taken, lowest and median winning bids</li>
        <li>A section that filled only shows that at least that many bid</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h2>What it adjusts for</h2>
      <ul class="bul">
        <li>Course, professor, time slot (early, evening, Friday)</li>
        <li>Seats on offer, and each bidding window</li>
        <li>Little history on a professor: it leans on the course, then the subject, then all courses</li>
        <li>Recent terms count more</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card" id="replay">
      <h2>“e$X → about 90%”</h2>
      <ul class="bul">
        <li>It estimates how many students bid, and how much each bids</li>
        <li>The window is replayed {FORECAST_RUNS.toLocaleString('en-US')} times; the highest bids get the seats</li>
        <li>e$X won a seat in about 90 of 100 replays</li>
        <li>Groups: every member needs a seat</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card" id="limits">
      <h2>What it can’t know</h2>
      <ul class="bul">
        <li>A new professor, or rule changes</li>
        <li>Drops and released seats</li>
        <li>What this term’s students decide</li>
        <li>Seats marked ≈ are estimates and can be wrong</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
  <p class="disc discs">Estimated from past bids; not a guarantee. Not affiliated with SMU.</p>

  <section id="accuracy" class="acc-sec">
    <h2>How accurate it is</h2>
    {latest.fMed !== undefined && <p class="acc-big">Median miss: {eS(latest.fMed)}</p>}

    <h3 id="accounts">What it accounts for</h3>
    <ul class="acc-points">
      {accounts.map((a) => <li><b>{a[0]}</b><span>{a[1]}</span></li>)}
    </ul>

    {bt.length > 0 && (
    <details id="accuracy-more" class="bt more">
      <summary>More accuracy detail</summary>
    <ul class="bul notes">
      <li>Average miss: how far our predicted clearing bid (the lowest bid that got in) was from the real one, on sections that filled.</li>
      <li>Accuracy is for forecasts made just before each window opens; forecasts for windows further ahead are less certain.</li>
      <li>Last term’s bid: the last term of the same type, for the same section, else the instructor, else the course.</li>
      <li>Our 80% range: the forecast’s 10th to 90th percentile. About 80% is the target.</li>
      <li>Weak spot: for sections with a middling chance of filling, it said “fills” more often than it happened.</li>
    </ul>
      <div class="inner">
      <p class="acc">Each test fits the model only on terms before the test term, then forecasts every section in every bidding window of that term. The model’s settings were tuned on an earlier term (2024-25 Term 2), so neither test term was used for tuning.</p>
      <h3>Sections that filled</h3>
    <div class="tbl t4" role="table" aria-label="Accuracy on sections that filled" id="accuracy-summary">
      <div class="th" role="row">
        <span role="columnheader">Test term</span>
        <span role="columnheader" class="r">Average miss</span>
        <span role="columnheader" class="r">Average miss, reusing last term’s bid</span>
        <span role="columnheader" class="r">Real bid inside our 80% range</span>
        <span role="columnheader" class="r">Filled sections</span>
      </div>
      {tests.map((t) => (
        <div class="tr" role="row">
          <span role="cell"><b>{t.term}</b></span>
          <span role="cell" class="r num" data-label="Average miss">{eS(t.fMae)}</span>
          <span role="cell" class="r num" data-label="Reusing last term’s bid">{eS(t.fBase)}</span>
          <span role="cell" class="r num" data-label="Inside our 80% range">{pct(t.fCov)}</span>
          <span role="cell" class="r num" data-label="Filled sections">{t.filled.toLocaleString('en-US')}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
      <h3>All forecasts</h3>
      <p class="acc">Most section-windows do not fill and clear at the minimum bid, where the miss is tiny. That keeps the average over all forecasts small, so read it alongside the filled-section figures above. Here the 80% range is checked with ties at the minimum bid broken at random, so it can land on exactly 80% when the forecast is well calibrated.</p>
      <div class="tbl t5" role="table" aria-label="Accuracy on all forecasts">
        <div class="th" role="row">
          <span role="columnheader">Test term</span>
          <span role="columnheader" class="r">Average miss, forecast</span>
          <span role="columnheader" class="r">Average miss, reusing last term’s bid</span>
          <span role="columnheader" class="r">Real bid inside 80% range</span>
          <span role="columnheader" class="r">Forecasts (section-windows)</span>
        </div>
        {tests.map((t) => (
          <div class="tr" role="row">
            <span role="cell"><b>{t.term}</b></span>
            <span role="cell" class="r num" data-label="Average miss, forecast">{e(t.mae)}</span>
            <span role="cell" class="r num" data-label="Average miss, reusing last term’s bid">{e(t.base)}</span>
            <span role="cell" class="r num" data-label="Real bid inside 80% range">{p(t.cov)}</span>
            <span role="cell" class="r num" data-label="Forecasts (section-windows)">{t.n.toLocaleString('en-US')}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="acc">The last-term baseline can only forecast sections that had a bid to reuse, so it is scored on fewer forecasts ({tests.map((t) => t.baseN.toLocaleString('en-US')).join(' and ')}). The filled-section misses compare both on the filled sections the baseline could forecast.</p>
      <h3>By window, 2025-26 Term 2</h3>
      <p class="acc">The average miss is across all forecasts in the window. The range and the sections tested count only sections that filled.</p>
      <AccuracyChart client:load byWindow={fm.byWindow} />
      <div class="tbl" role="table" aria-label="Backtest by window">
        <div class="th" role="row">
          <span role="columnheader">Window</span>
          <span role="columnheader" class="r">Real bid inside our range, filled sections</span>
          <span role="columnheader" class="r">Filled sections tested</span>
          <span role="columnheader" class="r">Average miss, all forecasts</span>
          <span role="columnheader" class="r">Reliable: ≥70% right on ≥50 filled sections</span>
        </div>
        {bt.map((r) => (
          <div class="tr" role="row">
            <span role="cell"><b>{r.w}</b></span>
            <span role="cell" class="r num" data-label="Real bid inside our range, filled sections">{r.coverage80Filled === null ? '–' : pct(r.coverage80Filled)}</span>
            <span role="cell" class="r num" data-label="Filled sections tested">{r.filled}</span>
            <span role="cell" class="r num" data-label="Average miss, all forecasts">{r.maeCents === null ? '–' : eS(r.maeCents)}</span>
            <span role="cell" class="r" data-label="Reliable">{r.confidence === 'ok' ? 'Yes' : <span class="caution">Use with caution</span>}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="acc wk"><b>Weak spots.</b> From Round 2 Window 3 onward few sections fill, so there is little to check the bid range against and those windows are marked low confidence. Their average misses are small; most sections there do not fill and clear at the minimum bid.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
    )}
  </section>

  <details id="formula" class="formula-box">
    <summary><span class="btn">See the formula</span></summary>
    <div class="inner"><Formula /></div>
  </details>

  <details id="drop-chance" class="more">
    <summary>Drop chance</summary>
    <div class="inner how">
      {drop.map((t) => <p>{t}</p>)}
    </div>
  </details>

  <details id="snipe" class="more">
    <summary>Drop and re-bid</summary>
    <div class="inner how">
      {snipe.map((t) => <p>{t}</p>)}
    </div>
  </details>

  <details id="swap-chance" class="more">
    <summary>Swap chance</summary>
    <div class="inner how">
      {swap.map((t) => <p>{t}</p>)}
    </div>
  </details>
  </>)}
  <section id="seats" class="seatsnote">
    <h2>Seats left</h2>
    <ul class="bul">
      <li>Live scan of BOSS class search; scan time shown beside each list</li>
      <li>≈ marks seats estimated from the previous window's results; can be wrong</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="disc">Check BOSS before you decide.</p>
  </section>
</Base>

<script>
  // A link such as /method#demand points inside a closed <details>: open it, then scroll to the target.
  const reveal = () => {
    const el = document.getElementById(decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1)));
    if (!el) return;
    for (let d = el.closest('details'); d; d = d.parentElement?.closest('details') ?? null) d.open = true;
    el.scrollIntoView();
  };
  reveal();
  addEventListener('hashchange', reveal);
</script>

<style>
  .head { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--s3); margin-bottom: var(--s3); }
  h1 { font-size: 30px; margin: 0; }
  h2 { font-size: 17px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0 0 var(--s3); }
  p { margin: 0; }
  .src { color: var(--muted); }
  .tbl { font-size: 16px; }
  .bt :global(.charts) { margin-bottom: var(--s5); }

  .hero { margin-bottom: var(--s4); display: grid; gap: var(--s3); align-items: center; }
  .hero svg { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; max-height: 190px; }
  .hero .mk circle { r: 24px; }
  .hero .mk text { font-size: 28px; }
  .legend { list-style: none; counter-reset: n; display: grid; gap: var(--s3); margin: 0; padding: 0; font-size: 16px; max-width: 70ch; }
  .legend li { counter-increment: n; padding-left: 36px; position: relative; line-height: 1.4; }
  .legend li b { display: block; font-weight: 600; }
  .legend li span { color: var(--muted); }
  .legend li::before {
    content: counter(n); position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--accent); color: var(--accent-ink); font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; text-align: center; line-height: 24px;
  }

  .plain { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: var(--s4); margin-bottom: var(--s4); }
  .card { background: var(--surface); border-radius: var(--r-lg); padding: var(--s4); display: grid; gap: var(--s2); align-content: start; min-width: 0; }
  .card h2 { margin: 0; }
  .card li, .card p { line-height: 1.4; font-size: var(--fs-sm); }
  .bul, .acc-points { margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: var(--s2); }
  .acc-points { list-style: none; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); gap: var(--s4); }
  .acc-points li { display: grid; gap: 2px; align-content: start; padding-top: var(--s2); border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); line-height: 1.4; }
  .acc-points span { color: var(--muted); }
  .acc-sec { margin-bottom: var(--s4); display: grid; gap: var(--s3); }
  .acc-sec h3 { font-size: 17px; font-weight: 600; margin: var(--s3) 0 0; }
  .acc-lines { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 var(--s3); padding: 0; display: grid; gap: var(--s3); }
  .acc-lines li { display: grid; gap: 2px; }
  .acc-big { font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.4; max-width: 60ch; }
  .acc-lines small { font-size: 13px; opacity: 0.75; }
  .notes { color: var(--muted); max-width: 70ch; }
  .discs { margin-bottom: var(--s5); }
  .bul { list-style: disc; padding-left: 1.2em; }
  .bul li { display: list-item; }
  .more { background: var(--surface); border-radius: var(--r-lg); margin-bottom: var(--s3); }
  .more > summary { cursor: pointer; padding: var(--s3) var(--s4); font-size: var(--fs-md); font-weight: 600; min-height: 44px; display: flex; align-items: center; }
  .more > summary:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--focus); outline-offset: -2px; }
  .more .inner { padding: 0 var(--s4) var(--s4); }
  section, .card, details { min-width: 0; scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--topbar-h) + var(--s4)); }
  .how { display: grid; gap: var(--s2); align-content: start; }
  .how p, .acc { line-height: 1.5; max-width: 70ch; }
  .acc { margin: 0 0 var(--s3); }
  .bt h3 { font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; margin: var(--s5) 0 var(--s2); }
  .bt .inner > h3:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .t5, .t4 { margin-bottom: var(--s3); }
  .formula-box { margin-bottom: var(--s3); }
  .formula-box > summary { cursor: pointer; list-style: none; padding: var(--s2) 0; min-height: 44px; display: flex; align-items: center; }
  .formula-box > summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .formula-box .btn { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; min-height: 44px; padding: 0 var(--s5); border-radius: var(--r-lg); background: var(--accent); color: var(--accent-ink); font-weight: 600; font-size: var(--fs-md); }
  .formula-box[open] .btn { background: var(--surface); color: var(--text); box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--rule-strong); }
  .formula-box > summary:focus-visible .btn { outline: 2px solid var(--focus); outline-offset: 2px; }
  .formula-box .inner { padding: var(--s3) 0 var(--s4); min-width: 0; }
  .wk { margin-top: var(--s3); }

  .tbl { display: grid; }
  .t4 .th, .t4 .tr { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.3fr) minmax(0, 0.8fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 0.9fr); }
  .th, .tr { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.3fr) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 0.8fr) minmax(0, 0.8fr) minmax(0, 1.3fr); column-gap: var(--s3); align-items: baseline; padding: 6px var(--s2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .th { font-size: 13px; color: var(--muted); align-items: end; line-height: 1.25; }
  .seatsnote { margin-top: var(--s4); max-width: 70ch; }
  .seatsnote { display: grid; gap: var(--s2); }
  .seatsnote h2 { margin: 0; }
  .disc { font-size: var(--fs-sm); color: var(--muted); }
  .r { text-align: right; }
  .caution { display: inline-block; padding: 1px 8px; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--accent-soft); color: var(--text); font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.4; }

  /* On phones each row becomes label/value lines, so five columns never overprint. */
  @media (max-width: 400px) {
    /* Visually hidden, not display:none, so screen readers still get the column headers. */
